CAT = "cdcfibServices"; SEG = "SRV"
# The four services under the Ministry of Interior and the Board that recruits for them.
# Static facts only; no officeholders. See sources.md.
Q = [
# --- The Board and the ministry ---
("Which body recruits, promotes and disciplines officers of the NSCDC, NCoS, FFS and NIS?",
 "The CDCFIB", ["The Police Service Commission", "The Federal Civil Service Commission", "The Federal Character Commission"],
 "The Civil Defence, Correctional, Fire and Immigration Services Board handles appointment, promotion and discipline in the four services. The Police Service Commission does the same job for the Nigeria Police Force only."),
("The four services overseen by the CDCFIB come under which federal ministry?",
 "Ministry of Interior", ["Ministry of Defence", "Ministry of Justice", "Ministry of Police Affairs"],
 "The NSCDC, the Correctional Service, the Fire Service and the Immigration Service are all paramilitary agencies of the Federal Ministry of Interior. The armed forces sit under Defence and the police under Police Affairs."),
("Who chairs the CDCFIB?",
 "The Minister of Interior", ["The Minister of Defence", "The Attorney General", "The Inspector General of Police"],
 "The Board has been chaired by the minister responsible for internal affairs since it was set up in 1986; today that is the Minister of Interior."),
("Which service was part of the board set up in 1986 but was moved out of it in 1992?",
 "Nigeria Customs Service", ["Nigeria Police Force", "Federal Road Safety Corps", "Nigerian Correctional Service"],
 "The board began in 1986 as the Customs, Immigration and Prisons Services Board. Customs was moved out in 1992, and Civil Defence and later the Fire Service were added."),
# --- NSCDC ---
("The NSCDC began in 1967, during the civil war, as a civil defence committee in which city?",
 "Lagos", ["Kaduna", "Enugu", "Ibadan"],
 "It started in May 1967 as the Lagos Civil Defence Committee, which taught residents how to protect themselves during the war. It grew into the Nigeria Security and Civil Defence Corps (1970 in most accounts) and became a national security body in 1984."),
("In which year did the NSCDC first get its own Act of the National Assembly?",
 "2003", ["1999", "1970", "2010"],
 "The NSCDC Act was signed into law in 2003 and amended in 2007 to widen the Corps' duties. Before 2003 it worked without an Act of the National Assembly; 1970 is the year most accounts give for its change from the Lagos committee."),
("Guarding oil pipelines and other critical national assets is a core duty of:",
 "The NSCDC", ["The NIS", "The FFS", "The NCoS"],
 "The NSCDC Act charges the Corps with protecting critical national infrastructure, including pipelines, from vandals and illegal bunkering. The other three services have no such duty."),
("Which service licenses and supervises private guard companies in Nigeria?",
 "The NSCDC", ["The NIS", "The NCoS", "The FFS"],
 "Under its Act the NSCDC approves, licenses and monitors private guard companies, and can recommend the withdrawal of a licence when a company breaks the rules."),
("The NSCDC's Agro Rangers unit was set up to protect:",
 "Farmers and farmlands", ["Seaports and jetties", "Courts and judges", "Forests and wildlife"],
 "Agro Rangers were created in 2016 at the request of the agriculture ministry to protect farmers, farmlands and agricultural investments from attack."),
("The head of the NSCDC holds the title:",
 "Commandant General", ["Controller General", "Inspector General", "Chief Marshal"],
 "The NSCDC is led by a Commandant General. The Correctional, Fire and Immigration services are each led by a Controller General, and the police by an Inspector General."),
("Corps Assistant is the junior rank title of which service?",
 "The NSCDC", ["The NIS", "The FFS", "The NCoS"],
 "The NSCDC's lowest cadre is the Assistant cadre, Corps Assistant III to Corps Assistant I, below the Inspector cadre and the officer cadres."),
("Working beside the police to protect lives and property during crises and disasters describes:",
 "The NSCDC", ["The NIS", "The NCoS", "The CDCFIB"],
 "The NSCDC Act gives the Corps a role in crisis management, disaster response and protecting lives and property, in partnership with the Nigeria Police Force. The Board itself is not an operational service."),
# --- NCoS ---
("Before 2019, the Nigerian Correctional Service was called:",
 "The Nigerian Prisons Service", ["The Nigerian Reformatory Corps", "The Federal Penal Service", "The National Custody Agency"],
 "The Nigerian Correctional Service Act 2019 renamed the Nigerian Prisons Service and moved its focus from punishment to correction and reintegration."),
("The Nigerian Correctional Service Act 2019 divides the service into:",
 "Custodial and non-custodial", ["Federal and state", "Male and female", "Pre-trial and post-trial"],
 "The Act creates a Custodial Service, which runs custodial centres, and a Non-Custodial Service, which runs community sentences, probation, parole and restorative justice."),
("Which of these is a non-custodial measure under the 2019 Act?",
 "Community service", ["Solitary confinement", "Remand in custody", "Maximum security"],
 "Community service lets an offender work unpaid for the public instead of going into a custodial centre. Remand and maximum security both keep a person in custody."),
("Under the 2019 Act, the facilities once called prisons are now called:",
 "Custodial centres", ["Reform camps", "Detention barracks", "Penal colonies"],
 "The Act renamed prisons as custodial centres, in line with the service's new name and its aim of correcting rather than only punishing."),
("An inmate whose case is still being heard in court is called:",
 "An awaiting-trial inmate", ["A convicted inmate", "A parolee", "A probationer"],
 "An awaiting-trial inmate has not been convicted and is held in custody until the case ends. A parolee has been released early under supervision, and a probationer was sentenced to supervision instead of custody."),
("Parole means an inmate is:",
 "Released early under supervision", ["Moved to a stricter centre", "Freed with no conditions", "Pardoned by the President"],
 "Parole is conditional early release: the person serves the rest of the sentence in the community under supervision and can be recalled for breaking the conditions. A pardon cancels the punishment altogether."),
("The 2019 Act puts the stress of correctional work on:",
 "Reforming and reintegrating offenders", ["Longer and harsher sentences", "Hard labour on public works", "Keeping offenders out of view"],
 "The Act aims to reform, rehabilitate and reintegrate offenders into society, through education, vocational training and non-custodial options."),
("Nigeria's first colonial prison, opened in 1872, was on Broad Street in:",
 "Lagos", ["Calabar", "Lokoja", "Badagry"],
 "Broad Street Prison in Lagos opened in 1872 and is regarded as the start of the modern prison system that became the Correctional Service."),
# --- NIS ---
("Before it became a separate department, immigration work in Nigeria was done by:",
 "The Nigeria Police", ["The Nigeria Customs Service", "The Nigerian Army", "The Ministry of Foreign Affairs"],
 "Immigration duties were handled by the police until 1958, when an Immigration Department was carved out of it. An Act of Parliament established the department on 1 August 1963."),
("Which law now governs the Nigeria Immigration Service?",
 "The Immigration Act 2015", ["The Immigration Act 1963", "The Aliens Decree 1977", "The Passports Act 1990"],
 "The Immigration Act 2015 repealed the 1963 Act and is the service's current legal basis. It also gave the NIS powers against migrant smuggling."),
("Which service issues the Nigerian passport?",
 "The Nigeria Immigration Service", ["The Ministry of Foreign Affairs", "The National Identity Commission", "The Nigeria Customs Service"],
 "Issuing passports and other travel documents is a duty of the NIS under the Immigration Act. NIMC issues the National Identification Number, not passports."),
("CERPAC, issued by the NIS, is a residence document for:",
 "Foreigners working in Nigeria", ["Nigerians living abroad", "Refugees in border camps", "Pilgrims leaving Nigeria"],
 "CERPAC is the Combined Expatriate Residence Permit and Aliens Card, which a foreign national needs to live and work legally in Nigeria."),
("Smuggling migrants across Nigeria's borders is an offence the NIS fights under:",
 "The Immigration Act 2015", ["The Fire Service Act", "The NSCDC Act", "The Correctional Service Act"],
 "The 2015 Act made migrant smuggling an offence and gave the NIS the duty to investigate and prosecute it."),
("Controlling who enters and leaves Nigeria at airports, seaports and land borders is the job of:",
 "The NIS", ["The NSCDC", "The FFS", "The NCoS"],
 "The NIS controls entry and exit at all ports and patrols the borders. Customs checks goods, while the NIS checks people and their travel documents."),
("Machine-readable electronic passports were introduced in Nigeria in:",
 "2007", ["1999", "2015", "2019"],
 "The NIS began issuing machine-readable e-passports, with a chip holding the holder's data, in 2007. 2015 is the year of the current Immigration Act."),
("Under the ECOWAS free movement protocol, a citizen of another ECOWAS state may visit Nigeria without a visa for up to:",
 "90 days", ["30 days", "60 days", "180 days"],
 "The 1979 ECOWAS protocol on free movement lets community citizens with valid travel documents stay up to 90 days without a visa. The NIS enforces it at the borders."),
# --- FFS ---
("The Federal Fire Service traces its origin to a fire brigade set up in 1901 in:",
 "Lagos", ["Calabar", "Port Harcourt", "Kano"],
 "Organised firefighting began in 1901 as the Lagos Fire Brigade, which mainly protected colonial property on Lagos Island."),
("Which law turned the Lagos brigade into a national Federal Fire Service?",
 "The Fire Service Act 1963", ["The Fire Brigade Ordinance 1901", "The Fire Safety Act 2015", "The Emergency Services Act 1999"],
 "Parliament passed the Fire Service Act in 1963, after independence, creating a nationwide Federal Fire Service. 1901 is when the Lagos brigade began, without such an Act."),
("Inspecting buildings and enforcing fire safety codes is a duty of:",
 "The Federal Fire Service", ["The Immigration Service", "The Correctional Service", "The Civil Defence Corps"],
 "Besides fighting fires and carrying out rescues, the FFS sets fire prevention policy and enforces fire safety codes in buildings."),
("Which pair of duties belongs to the Federal Fire Service?",
 "Firefighting and rescue", ["Border patrol and visas", "Custody and parole", "Pipeline and farm patrols"],
 "The FFS fights fires and rescues people trapped in fires, collapses and crashes. Border work is the NIS, custody and parole the NCoS, and pipeline and farm patrols the NSCDC."),
]
